// The same rule, failing quietly rather than loudly. A wider integer needs no
// frame address to go wrong: it is held in a register, so the `ret` truncates
// it, and `300` leaves as `44`. Nothing reports the narrowing, because the
// narrowing is not written in the program -- it is what the entry point's
// convention does to whatever it is given.
fn main() -> Int64 {
    300
}
